<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Semantic similarity</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As outlined in §<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Introducing the features ‣ 3 Setting the stage: definitions and features ‣ Toward Understanding the Transferability of Adversarial Suffixes in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we aim to determine whether the semantic similarity between the embeddings of two prompts <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is predictive of the transferability of a suffix originally optimized for <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Statistical analysis setup.</span> We set up a quantitative framework for estimating the effect of semantic similarity (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mbox{sim}_{pp^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>sim</mtext><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mbox{sim}_{pp^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#S3.Ex2" title="Definition 3 (Semantic similarity) ‣ 3.3 Formal definitions ‣ 3 Setting the stage: definitions and features ‣ Toward Understanding the Transferability of Adversarial Suffixes in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) on transferability.
For models with multiple suffixes per prompt (Qwen, Llama 3.2), we fit a linear regression model, which predicts the fraction of the average transfer success of a prompt pair <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,p^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,p^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, we predict <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{pp^{\prime}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{pp^{\prime}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> from the feature vector <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{pp^{\prime}}:=[1,\cos(E(p),E(p^{\prime}))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{pp^{\prime}}:=[1,\cos(E(p),E(p^{\prime}))]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{pp^{\prime}}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{pp^{\prime}}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if all suffixes optimized for <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> jailbreak <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and vice versa. The features are standardized to have mean 0 and variance 1.
For models for which we have a single suffix per prompt (Vicuna, Llama 2), we fit an ordinal logistic regression model on the same feature vector but on <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{pp^{\prime}}\in\{0,0.5,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{pp^{\prime}}\in\{0,0.5,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>—0 if neither of the suffixes transfers, 0.5 if only one does, and 1 if both do.
Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2 Semantic similarity ‣ 5 Analysis of the factors correlated with transfer ‣ Toward Understanding the Transferability of Adversarial Suffixes in Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the resulting regression coefficients; following the standard statistical rules-of-thumb <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen, 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2010</a>)</cite>, we conclude that the effect sizes are small.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">

